WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:04.870
Even diehard Mortal Kombat fans are likely
to miss some of the details included in the

00:00:04.870 --> 00:00:06.620
latest live-action movie.

00:00:06.620 --> 00:00:08.390
These are the Easter eggs you may have missed.

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:14.010
From series standbys like Liu Kang and Sonya
Blade to more obscure fighters such as Nitara

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:19.240
and Reiko, Mortal Kombat manages to incorporate
a wide array of characters from the long-running

00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:20.269
game series.

00:00:20.269 --> 00:00:23.249
And that's only to mention those who actually
appear in the film.

00:00:23.249 --> 00:00:27.320
If you pay close attention, you'll notice
that even more characters get referenced throughout

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:30.130
the movie, even though they aren't seen on
screen.

00:00:30.130 --> 00:00:35.070
Early on, Sonya shows Cole Young her research
on the Mortal Kombat tournament and Outworld.

00:00:35.070 --> 00:00:38.640
One of the documents is a page from a book
that includes an illustration of a Native

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:41.390
American man with the caption "Matoka warrior."

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.980
The person in the illustration looks remarkably
like Nightwolf, a fighter introduced in Mortal

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:49.780
Kombat 3 who's a member of the fictional Matoka
tribe.

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:53.830
Sonya's research also includes an image of
what appears to be an Aztec bust, which could

00:00:53.830 --> 00:00:55.540
be a nod to Kotal Kahn.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:59.690
Mortal Kombat X introduced the intimidating
character whose design draws some obvious

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:02.010
inspiration from Aztec culture.

00:01:02.010 --> 00:01:06.630
Elsewhere in the movie, characters namedrop
Shao Kahn, the brutal Emperor of Outworld,

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:10.600
as well as Bo' Rai Cho, the martial arts grandmaster
who trained Liu Kang.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:14.640
Raiden's temple also contains a couple of
visual nods to unseen characters.

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:19.049
The signature fan blades of the Edenian Princess
Kitana can be seen displayed in one of the

00:01:19.049 --> 00:01:23.630
temple's corridors, and one crucial scene
sees Kano stealing an item that looks very

00:01:23.630 --> 00:01:25.670
similar to the Amulet of Shinnok.

00:01:25.670 --> 00:01:30.460
The appropriately named magical talisman in
question belongs to Shinnok, an elder god

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:32.259
who first appeared in Mortal Kombat 4.

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:36.700
Not only does the new Mortal Kombat movie
make numerous references to characters who

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:41.350
don't even appear in the film, but it also
pushes things even further by paying homage

00:01:41.350 --> 00:01:42.990
to the series' creators.

00:01:42.990 --> 00:01:47.090
We get our first taste of this when Jax introduces
himself to Cole Young.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:50.979
Jax mentions a past fight where Cole managed
to take the belt away from a fighter named

00:01:50.979 --> 00:01:56.259
"Eddie Tobias," a subtle nod to Mortal Kombat
co-creators Ed Boon and John Tobias.

00:01:56.259 --> 00:02:00.819
Tobias is responsible for much of the original
character design, lore, and world-building

00:02:00.819 --> 00:02:02.259
of the early games.

00:02:02.259 --> 00:02:06.670
Additionally, the movie honors the Mortal
Kombat franchise's real home: Chicago.

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:11.129
When Cole's relation to Scorpion is revealed,
the protagonist finds it hard to believe.

00:02:11.129 --> 00:02:14.470
He considers himself just a kid from the Southside
of Chicago.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:18.840
The Windy City was the home of original Mortal
Kombat developer Midway Games, which was also

00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:22.890
responsible for other arcade hits like Rampage
and NBA Jam.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:28.060
Midway Games filed for bankruptcy in 2009;
Warner Bros. bought the Mortal Kombat IP that

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:29.060
same year.

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:33.260
Chicago can still claim Mortal Kombat because
NetherRealm Studios, the studio responsible

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:36.629
for the current crop of games in the franchise,
is based in the city.

00:02:37.629 --> 00:02:41.830
Colors play a huge role in Mortal Kombat,
especially the older games in the series.

00:02:41.830 --> 00:02:45.890
Much of the ninja characters like Scorpion,
Sub-Zero, and Rain were different from each

00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:49.579
other visually thanks to the primary colors
used in their design.

00:02:49.579 --> 00:02:53.180
It only makes sense for the new movie to play
around with this concept in its own unique

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:54.180
way.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.530
When we're first introduced to Cole Young,
he's seen prepping his hands for an MMA fight,

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:00.180
wrapping them yellow tape.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:05.269
This is a reference to Scorpion and his clan,
the Shirai Ryu, whose main color is yellow.

00:03:05.269 --> 00:03:09.120
It's also an early hint that Cole is somehow
related to the clan and Scorpion.

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.550
There's even a spot of yellow on the strap
of his gym bag.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:16.530
Not to be outdone by his rival, Sub-Zero also
provides us with a major visual clue that

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:18.439
hints at his ultimate fate.

00:03:18.439 --> 00:03:22.220
During his final battle with Scorpion and
Cole, the ice-cold assassin gets rid of the

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:25.660
blue parts of his outfit showing himself in
complete black.

00:03:25.660 --> 00:03:30.379
This is a reference to the super-dark Noob
Saibot, a character that the first Sub-Zero,

00:03:30.379 --> 00:03:34.200
Bi-Han, later becomes after being killed by
Scorpion in the games.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:38.360
Noob Saibot is a play on the reversed last
names of Ed Boon and John Tobias.

00:03:38.360 --> 00:03:42.120
The character was introduced in the series,
thanks to Boon, in Mortal Kombat II.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:47.450
However, Noob Saibot was so secret a character
that not even Boon's partner Tobias knew about

00:03:47.450 --> 00:03:48.450
him at first.

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:52.840
Tobias was already a few months into development
on Mortal Kombat 3 before he learned about

00:03:52.840 --> 00:03:54.459
the secret character's existence.

00:03:55.459 --> 00:03:59.400
Mortal Kombat movie wouldn't be considered
authentic without the iconic techno theme

00:03:59.400 --> 00:04:01.630
that has become associated with the series.

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:05.299
Fortunately, there are two scenes in the new
film where bits of it can be heard.

00:04:05.299 --> 00:04:09.879
When Liu Kang launches his trademark bicycle
kick against Kabal, a few familiar notes play

00:04:09.879 --> 00:04:10.879
in the background.

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:14.920
It's subtle, especially compared to when the
theme makes its second appearance during the

00:04:14.920 --> 00:04:16.780
film's conclusive stretch.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:21.940
When Scorpion finally appears to face Sub-Zero
in their ensuing battle, the theme returns,

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.169
this time booming in an impressive remix.

00:04:24.169 --> 00:04:28.570
The Mortal Kombat theme is officially called
"Techno Syndrome," created by Belgian duo

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:33.400
The Immortals, also known as Maurice "Praga
Khan" Engelen and Oliver Adams.

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:36.530
The two are also known for their work from
the group Lords of Acid.

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:41.620
Midway Games contacted Engelen and Adams about
making an album to go along with the 1994

00:04:41.620 --> 00:04:44.000
home console release of the hit arcade game.

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.310
While between tours, the two got to work producing
the entire record in four weeks.

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:52.531
Mortal Kombat: The Album featured songs based
on the original roster of characters, but

00:04:52.531 --> 00:04:55.210
it was Techno Syndrome that became the defining
hit.

00:04:55.210 --> 00:05:00.040
It went on to be used in commercials, movies,
and just about anything the series has dipped

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:01.410
its brutal hands in.

00:05:01.410 --> 00:05:07.010
For the soundtrack of the 2021 movie, composer
Benjamin Wallfisch gave the theme a new EDM-tinged

00:05:07.010 --> 00:05:08.010
feel.

00:05:09.010 --> 00:05:12.380
Early on in the film, Sonya fills Cole in
on the legend of the Mortal Kombat tournament

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:16.750
by showing him her collection of newspaper
clippings and other research materials.

00:05:16.750 --> 00:05:24.139
"Throughout history, different cultures all
over the world reference a great tournament."

00:05:24.139 --> 00:05:27.310
One obscured headline includes the text "storm
causes panic."

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:31.449
Although the first word is mostly cut away,
it seems possible that the whole phrase is

00:05:31.449 --> 00:05:33.919
meant to read "lightning storm causes panic."

00:05:33.919 --> 00:05:38.260
Considering it's on Sonya's wall of research,
that could be a reference to Lord Raiden,

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:42.669
who arrives in Earthrealm via an intense series
of lightning strikes on multiple occasions.

00:05:42.669 --> 00:05:46.819
The headline could be describing what Raiden's
strikes looked like to normal Earth-dwellers

00:05:46.819 --> 00:05:50.300
who were unaware that they were witnessing
the approach of the God of Thunder.

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:53.860
Similarly, another incomplete headline ends
with "breaking hailstorm."

00:05:53.860 --> 00:05:58.720
Presumably, it's meant to say "record-breaking
hailstorm," which could be a reference to

00:05:58.720 --> 00:05:59.720
Sub-Zero.

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:03.740
Earlier in the movie, he arrived in Earthrealm
and manifested a fierce ice storm while attempting

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:04.740
to kill Cole.

00:06:04.740 --> 00:06:09.379
Could he have done something similar at another
time, leading to people confusing his supernatural

00:06:09.379 --> 00:06:11.470
powers for an extra intense weather event?

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:16.210
Mortal Kombat is a game series that isn't
afraid to make changes to its own canon.

00:06:16.210 --> 00:06:21.069
For instance, the ninth title in the franchise,
also titled Mortal Kombat, was a reboot for

00:06:21.069 --> 00:06:25.160
the series and made some key changes in the
stories of the first three games.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:28.919
The new movie is just as happy to play around
with established lore.

00:06:28.919 --> 00:06:33.320
Whether it be Kano teaming up with the heroes,
or introducing a completely new protagonist

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:34.320
to the fold.

00:06:34.320 --> 00:06:38.410
But things get a little ridiculous when it
comes to Jax and how he loses his arms.

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:41.770
Sub-Zero is the one to take away Jax's human
arms in the new movie.

00:06:41.770 --> 00:06:46.349
But this is only the latest in a variety of
unfortunate ways Jax has lost his arms in

00:06:46.349 --> 00:06:47.470
the game series.

00:06:47.470 --> 00:06:52.389
In the ninth game, Jax has his arms exploded
when Ermac uses his telekinetic powers.

00:06:52.389 --> 00:06:56.979
In the web series Mortal Kombat: Legacy, grenades
destroy Jax's arms.

00:06:56.979 --> 00:07:01.909
And in the animated Mortal Kombat Legends:
Scorpion's Revenge, Goro just completely rips

00:07:01.909 --> 00:07:02.909
them off.

00:07:02.909 --> 00:07:07.039
Let's hope that the next retelling of how
Jax loses his arms is kinder to the Special

00:07:07.039 --> 00:07:08.220
Forces Soldier.

00:07:09.220 --> 00:07:13.130
Mortal Kombat more than earns its R rating
thanks to the graphic fatalities that conclude

00:07:13.130 --> 00:07:14.699
many of the brawls in the movie.

00:07:14.699 --> 00:07:19.970
In fact, the film actually recreates several
of the iconic finishing moves from the game

00:07:19.970 --> 00:07:20.970
franchise.

00:07:20.970 --> 00:07:24.949
Early in the movie, an encounter between Kano
and Reptile ends with Kano reaching into the

00:07:24.949 --> 00:07:27.440
creature's chest and ripping out its heart.

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:32.120
That same move was used as Kano's fatality
in the very first Mortal Kombat game.

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:36.470
Similarly, the fire-breathing finisher that
Scorpion pulls on Sub-Zero, is a throwback

00:07:36.470 --> 00:07:37.700
to the original game.

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:41.980
Two of the movie's goriest moments also come
directly from the source material.

00:07:41.980 --> 00:07:45.860
Kung Lao's hat is the butt of quite a few
jokes throughout the film, but he proves its

00:07:45.860 --> 00:07:50.960
brutal utility when he quickly ends an encounter
with the winged Nitara by turning the bladed

00:07:50.960 --> 00:07:53.631
headwear into a buzz saw and feeding her into
it.

00:07:53.631 --> 00:07:59.060
A version of this particularly gruesome fatality
was first used in the spin-off game Mortal

00:07:59.060 --> 00:08:04.030
Kombat: Shaolin Monks, and has gone on to
become a mainstay of Kung Lao's repertoire

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:05.449
in the main series.

00:08:05.449 --> 00:08:09.970
Meanwhile, Jax gets the chance to test out
his new arcana-powered metal arms while fighting

00:08:09.970 --> 00:08:10.970
Reiko.

00:08:10.970 --> 00:08:15.560
After beating Shang Tsung's lackey to his
knees, Jax claps his hands on Reiko's head,

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:17.080
bursting it like a melon.

00:08:17.080 --> 00:08:21.629
This move was one of Jax's first fatalities
when the character made his debut in Mortal

00:08:21.629 --> 00:08:22.891
Kombat II.

00:08:22.891 --> 00:08:24.891
Liu Kane gets an opportunity to show off his

00:08:24.891 --> 00:08:28.607
impressive arcana power as well, summoning a giant dragon

00:08:28.607 --> 00:08:30.607
made of fire to fatally burn Kabal.

00:08:31.550 --> 00:08:36.750
This is a variation of Liu Kang's dragon transformation
fatality that he first started using in the

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:38.370
franchise's second installment.

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:43.190
Longtime fans of the franchise are no doubt
going to be thrilled that Mortal Kombat is

00:08:43.190 --> 00:08:46.850
full of small references to the particulars
of the video games.

00:08:46.850 --> 00:08:51.630
Take, for instance, Kung Lao's words of encouragement
to Cole and Kano when they begin their training:

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:53.829
"All you can do now is test your might."

00:08:53.829 --> 00:08:58.350
This is a nod to the first game in the series,
which featured a mini-game called "Test Your

00:08:58.350 --> 00:08:59.350
Might."

00:08:59.350 --> 00:09:03.190
It tasked players with mashing buttons to
make their characters chop through piles of

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:07.420
material ranging from planks of wood to a
block of pure diamond.

00:09:07.420 --> 00:09:11.760
The filmmakers also managed to incorporate
other bits of now-iconic dialogue players

00:09:11.760 --> 00:09:15.490
would regularly hear from the game's announcer
at the end of matches.

00:09:15.490 --> 00:09:19.490
Several of the fight scenes in the movie conclude
with the characters saying things like "flawless

00:09:19.490 --> 00:09:21.410
victory" and "fatality."

00:09:21.410 --> 00:09:24.380
After he defeats Reptile, Kano even giddily
announced:

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:25.560
"Kano wins."

00:09:25.560 --> 00:09:28.040
These references aren't just relegated to
dialogue.

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:32.170
The set design of Mortal Kombat draws some
inspiration from the levels of the games,

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:34.700
specifically the iconic stage known as the
Pit.

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:39.220
There have been several versions of this arena
throughout the franchise, but the basic gist

00:09:39.220 --> 00:09:43.850
is always the same: The Kombatants fight on
a bridge overlooking a pit of razor-sharp

00:09:43.850 --> 00:09:44.850
spikes.

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:48.880
A version of this concept appears late in
the movie when Jax squares off against Reiko.

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:53.740
Fatalities aren't the only moves from the
Mortal Kombat games that pop up in the film.

00:09:53.740 --> 00:09:57.920
Cole's spirited daughter Emily frequently
tells her father to use an uppercut during

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:02.860
his fights, a punch that has been ubiquitous
for nearly every character in the series starting

00:10:02.860 --> 00:10:04.260
with the first game.

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:09.370
During a 2016 interview with Game Informer,
series co-creator Ed Boon even said that nailing

00:10:09.370 --> 00:10:12.270
the uppercut was integral in the development
of the first game.

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:14.930
He recalled,
"The big thing was this uppercut.

00:10:14.930 --> 00:10:19.161
Once we got this uppercut going ... suddenly
everybody is coming into my office, 'Aw, let

00:10:19.161 --> 00:10:20.670
me see the game!'"

00:10:20.670 --> 00:10:24.000
Another frequently used maneuver from the
original games that got a special callout

00:10:24.000 --> 00:10:25.560
in the movie is the leg sweep.

00:10:25.560 --> 00:10:29.180
In what appears to be a nod to players who
love to spam the move in order to score an

00:10:29.180 --> 00:10:34.639
easy victory, Liu Kang leg-sweeps Kano several
times during one of their training sessions.

00:10:34.639 --> 00:10:38.800
After Kano successfully hops over one of the
sweeps, Liu Kang then fakes him out before

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:40.380
getting him with another one.

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:44.200
This will no doubt feel familiar to anyone
who has thrown their controller in frustration

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.030
because their friend wouldn't stop using the
move.

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:50.750
And, of course, many of the characters get
a chance to show off their special abilities.

00:10:50.750 --> 00:10:55.680
Liu Kang gets his fireballs, Kano has his
laser eye, and even Sonya's pink energy rings

00:10:55.680 --> 00:10:56.680
make an appearance.

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:01.399
When the characters got revealed for the latest
Mortal Kombat movie, we all had one burning

00:11:01.399 --> 00:11:03.670
question: Where is Johnny Cage?

00:11:03.670 --> 00:11:07.880
Mortal Kombat producer Todd Garner announced
the sad news that the character wouldn't appear

00:11:07.880 --> 00:11:11.720
in the film, but gave hope of his appearance
in a potential sequel.

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:16.240
And now, a huge Easter Egg has ignited our
hopes for Cage's return to the big screen.

00:11:16.240 --> 00:11:20.370
The movie ends with Shang Tsung's evil crew
defeated, but Raiden has requested that our

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:22.950
heroes seek out new defenders for Earthrealm.

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:27.060
As Cole leaves his MMA gym on this mission
from the Thunder God, he reveals that his

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:28.779
search is taking him to Hollywood.

00:11:28.779 --> 00:11:34.459
We then get an image of a poster for a movie
titled Citizen Cage, starring actor-turned-Kombatant

00:11:34.459 --> 00:11:35.459
Johnny Cage.

00:11:35.459 --> 00:11:39.440
Johnny Cage is a staple character for the
game series and even shares the spotlight

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:43.610
with his daughter Cassie Cage in Mortal Kombat
X and Mortal Kombat 11.

00:11:43.610 --> 00:11:48.839
With such a big personality and his trademark
split punch, Johnny is, unsurprisingly, modeled

00:11:48.839 --> 00:11:52.010
after a real Hollywood action star: Jean-Claude
Van Damme.

00:11:52.010 --> 00:11:56.560
The minds behind the IP wanted Van Damme to
be in what eventually became Mortal Kombat,

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:57.990
but he wasn't interested.

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:00.800
Thus, Ed Boon and John Tobias forged ahead.

00:12:00.800 --> 00:12:05.320
The project turned into Mortal Kombat, and
Johnny Cage was born.

00:12:05.320 --> 00:12:06.840
